00:37:02.800 --> 00:37:04.040
And then we would have cried.

749
00:37:04.040 --> 00:37:06.000
And then we'd laugh hysterically.

750
00:37:06.200 --> 00:37:08.560
We'd look like crazy people.

751
00:37:08.700 --> 00:37:09.920
But we would have had fun.

752
00:37:10.220 --> 00:37:12.160
I tell you what, we would have been good TV.

753
00:37:12.740 --> 00:37:13.640
Oh, my God.

754
00:37:13.640 --> 00:37:15.060
We would have been great TV.

755
00:37:17.060 --> 00:37:17.800
OK, OK.

756
00:37:17.980 --> 00:37:21.060
Well, we actually asked Greg about this Amazing Race moment.

757
00:37:21.260 --> 00:37:22.460
And here's what he had to say.

758
00:37:22.460 --> 00:37:27.240
The comparison to Amazing Race was fun because it's a reality.

759
00:37:27.500 --> 00:37:29.980
We were a reality based comedy show.

760
00:37:30.160 --> 00:37:36.540
It was a lot easier for the visual look of the show to be able to try and do references

761
00:37:36.540 --> 00:37:40.380
to other reality shows rather than movies, for instance.

762
00:37:40.380 --> 00:37:48.260
So there's like a Titanic reference in Booze Cruise, but I don't think it works as visually

763
00:37:48.260 --> 00:37:52.540
well as Amazing Race reference for here.

764
00:37:53.260 --> 00:37:53.860
Yeah.

765
00:37:54.200 --> 00:37:56.160
It totally makes sense to me, though.

766
00:37:56.300 --> 00:38:00.400
I think Michael is such a fan of pop culture and television and film.

767
00:38:00.560 --> 00:38:06.880
Like, he wants any opportunity for his documentary of this paper company, whatever this is, this

768
00:38:06.880 --> 00:38:12.080
camera crew that follows him around, he thinks he's on par with the entertainment of the day.

769
00:38:12.320 --> 00:38:12.800
Yes.

770
00:38:13.200 --> 00:38:13.680
Yes.

771
00:38:14.280 --> 00:38:19.320
Well, so now everyone gets in their cars and we kind of start driving around.

772
00:38:19.560 --> 00:38:25.360
And Angela, speaking of driving, we had a lot of questions about how we pulled off these

773
00:38:25.360 --> 00:38:26.200
driving shots.

774
00:38:27.060 --> 00:38:35.680
Amy Jackson, DeAndra Tassone, Holly E., TJ McMillan, William Burke, and many more people all asked,

775
00:38:36.160 --> 00:38:38.920
how did filming work for the driving scenes?

776
00:38:39.060 --> 00:38:42.400
Are you guys actually driving or is there a green screen?

777
00:38:43.020 --> 00:38:45.160
What cameras were used in the car scenes?

778
00:38:45.360 --> 00:38:47.960
And are the actors really driving around?

779
00:38:48.680 --> 00:38:51.320
Well, first of all, the actors really are driving around.

780
00:38:51.460 --> 00:38:52.520
That is not a green screen.

781
00:38:52.780 --> 00:38:57.620
And then we went to Greg and we asked him to talk a little bit about what it was like to

782
00:38:57.620 --> 00:38:58.840
shoot people in the cars.

783
00:38:58.840 --> 00:39:00.180
And here's what he had to say.

784
00:39:00.180 --> 00:39:08.040
And one of the things I had noticed in production was how easy it was to shoot in a car for our

785
00:39:08.040 --> 00:39:08.380
show.

786
00:39:08.560 --> 00:39:13.320
You just basically put the two characters in the car and set up lipstick cameras and drove

787
00:39:13.320 --> 00:39:14.900
around and improvised a lot.

788
00:39:15.060 --> 00:39:21.880
And so the idea for a traveling salesman was we could get a lot of production value out of

789
00:39:21.880 --> 00:39:24.820
getting out of the office and it wouldn't be too hard for us to do.

790
00:39:24.940 --> 00:39:28.980
We just tricked out these cars and they would go off.

791
00:39:28.980 --> 00:39:33.920
So we have a little lingo alert there when Greg talks about lipstick cameras.

792
00:39:34.320 --> 00:39:41.900
Those are itty bitty tiny cameras that they would put up in our visors in the car at an

793
00:39:41.900 --> 00:39:42.320
angle.

794
00:39:42.460 --> 00:39:46.500
And one would shoot one passenger and one would shoot the other passenger.

795
00:39:47.000 --> 00:39:50.280
And we would just literally do what Greg said.

796
00:39:50.460 --> 00:39:54.440
We would just drive around and we would have a little walkie talkie in the car.

797
00:39:54.440 --> 00:39:59.500
And there would be a car either in front of us or behind us that had the crew, the director,

798
00:39:59.760 --> 00:40:00.600
a sound person.

799
00:40:01.080 --> 00:40:02.260
They could hear us.

800
00:40:02.360 --> 00:40:06.280
They could see us on a little like mobile video screen.

801
00:40:06.920 --> 00:40:09.200
And they would talk to us over the walkie talkie.

802
00:40:09.360 --> 00:40:10.760
And they would say, oh, that was great.

803
00:40:10.760 --> 00:40:11.680
We're going to do that again.

804
00:40:12.300 --> 00:40:14.500
And then we would just drive around.

805
00:40:14.700 --> 00:40:15.800
They would have police cars.

806
00:40:15.940 --> 00:40:17.200
Remember the police cars, Ange?

807
00:40:17.640 --> 00:40:22.540
Yeah, we had like the motor motorcycle police guys, too, who would sort of be in the front

808
00:40:22.540 --> 00:40:27.180
and we'd have this path that was cleared for us so that, you know, we wouldn't have to

809
00:40:27.180 --> 00:40:30.680
drive or around people who didn't know we were filming.

810
00:40:30.860 --> 00:40:30.960
Right.

811
00:40:31.060 --> 00:40:31.920
So it was safe.

812
00:40:32.620 --> 00:40:34.680
And we would just basically make a loop.

813
00:40:34.680 --> 00:40:38.380
Usually in one neighborhood, we'd just make the same loop over and over.

814
00:40:39.000 --> 00:40:42.020
Yeah, that was a whole thing that Kent Sabornak, a.k.a.

815
00:40:42.140 --> 00:40:44.160
Kentopedia, he would have to clear that.

816
00:40:44.440 --> 00:40:47.240
He'd have to call the highway patrol or the police.

817
00:40:47.760 --> 00:40:53.500
And they would give us a zone that we were allowed to use for a certain period of time on that day.

818
00:40:54.300 --> 00:40:56.360
And we would just loop around.

819
00:40:56.740 --> 00:41:01.380
And it was always difficult in Los Angeles to find a loop that didn't have any palm trees.

820
00:41:01.920 --> 00:41:08.220
So we tend to film in the same neighborhoods over and over because clearly they found trees that

821
00:41:08.220 --> 00:41:11.800
looked possibly East Coast-ish, you know?

822
00:41:12.340 --> 00:41:12.600
Yes.

823
00:41:12.600 --> 00:41:15.020
We got to know these loops very well.

824
00:41:15.420 --> 00:41:20.560
One of the things Dave Rogers said in the DVD commentary is that this was the first time that

825
00:41:20.560 --> 00:41:22.840
we used the lipstick cameras in the cars.

826
00:41:23.140 --> 00:41:26.660
Previously, when we had done driving scenes, one of our camera operators,

827
00:41:26.900 --> 00:41:30.300
Randall or Matt, would be scrunched in the back seat with a camera.

828
00:41:30.300 --> 00:41:35.120
And, you know, I noticed the video quality was a little different on the lipstick cameras

829
00:41:35.120 --> 00:41:36.420
when I was watching it.

830
00:41:36.580 --> 00:41:41.120
It's not quite as crisp as a Matt camera or a Randall camera.

831
00:41:41.720 --> 00:41:43.480
Look at you, Miss Techie.

832
00:41:43.660 --> 00:41:45.000
You got a tech alert there?

833
00:41:45.160 --> 00:41:45.720
Look at you.

834
00:41:45.960 --> 00:41:46.300
I noticed it.

835
00:41:46.460 --> 00:41:47.300
I did.

